<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-10-3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Natural Base</title>

<assemblage component="instructor">
<p>In this Lesson we briefly introduce the natural exponential and natural log functions. We consider their graphs and solving equations, and we write exponential growth and decay functions with base <m>e</m>.</p>

<p>GOALS</p>
<p><ul>
    <li><p>Graph exponential functions </p></li>   
    <li><p>Solve exponential equations</p></li>
    <li><p>Convert exponential functions to base <m>e</m></p></li>
</ul></p></assemblage>

<activity><title>Base <m>e</m></title>

<p><ol marker="a.">
<li><p>Recall that <m>e</m> is an irrational number approximately equal to 2.7. Use your calculator to help you complete the table. Round values to tenths.</p>

        <aside component="instructor"><p>1(a) Students are not comfortable with <m>e</m>, because they are not comfortable with any irrational numbers. Take every opportunity to state that <m>e</m> is about 2.7.</p></aside>

        <tabular top="minor" halign="center" right="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor">              
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>f(x)=2^x</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>g(x) = e^x</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>h(x) = 3^x</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>-1.0</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>-0.5</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>0</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>0.5</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>1.0</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>1.5</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>2.0</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>2.5</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>3.0</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
        </tabular></li>

<li><p>Plot points from the table to sketch all three graphs on the grid below.</p>
        <image source="act-10-3-1.png" width = "55%"></image><description>grid -2 t0 10 by -2 t0 10</description></li>

<li><p>On the same grid, graph the inverse functions
    <me>~y = \log_2 {x},~y = \ln {x},~~ \text{and}~ ~y = \log_3 {x}</me>.</p>

        <aside component="instructor"><p>1(c) Many students read "ln x" as "I(eye)-n-x." Point out that "ln" stands for natural log, so it's an l not an I(eye).</p></aside></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<activity><title>Exponential Growth and Decay</title>

<introduction><p>It is useful to express an exponential growth or decay law with base <m>e</m>. In other words, to replace <m>~P(t) = P_0 b^t~</m> by <m>~P(t) = P_0 e^{kt}</m>. This is easy to do.</p>

<p>First, notice that the laws of exponents tell us that <m>~(e^k)^t = e^{kt}~</m>.</p></introduction>  

<p><ol marker="a.">

<li><p>Follow the steps to write <m>~P = 25(\alert{10})^t~</m> as <m>~P = 25 (\alert{e^k})^t~</m>:</p> 
    <p><ol marker="1.">
    <li><p>We need to replace <m>10</m> by <m>e^k</m>. That is, we need to find <m>k</m> so that <m>~e^k=10~</m>.
       Solve this equation for <m>k</m>. Round your answer to tenths.</p></li>
    <li><p>Substitute your value for <m>k</m> into the formula for <m>P</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p>Check that the two formulas for <m>P</m> give the same outputs for <m>x = 1,~x=2,~</m> and <m>x=3</m>.</p>

            <aside component="instructor"><p>2(b) It is very helpful to demonstrate that both forms of the function have the same graph.</p></aside></li>
    </ol></p></li>

<li><p>Rewrite each exponential function with base <m>e</m>:</p>
  <p><ol cols="2" marker="1.">
    <li><p><m>H = 12(1.05)^t</m></p></li>
    <li><p><m>N = 80(0.16)^t</m></p></li>
  </ol></p>
    
    <p>How do you know from the formulas that the second function is decreasing?</p></li>


<li><p>Under favorable conditions, a colony of the bacteria E. coli can grow as <m>~P(t) = P_0 e^{0.0344t}</m>, where  is measured in minutes.</p>
    <p><ol marker="1.">
    <li><p> Convert the growth law to the form <m>~P(t) = P_0 b^t</m>.</p></li> 

    <li><p>What is the percent growth rate for E. coli?</p></li>

    <li><p>Find the doubling time for E. coli.</p></li> 
</ol></p></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<activity><title>What is <m>e</m> ?</title>

<introduction><p>You have already met <m>\pi</m>, an irrational number that turns up in many mathematical models, including the geometric formulas you know. It is one of the constants that describes our universe. Another such universal constant is <m>e</m>, but its significance is not usually apparent without calculus. Here is a preview.</p></introduction>

<p><ol marker="a.">
    <li><p>Use your calculator to evaluate the expression <m>\left(1+\dfrac{1}{n}\right)^n</m> for large values of <m>n</m>.  What number does the expression approach as <m>n</m> increases?</p>

            <tabular top="minor" halign="center" right="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor">              
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><m>n</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>100</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>1000</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>10,000</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>100,000</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>\left(1+\dfrac{1}{n}\right)^n</m></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell></row>
            </tabular></li>

    <li><p>Recall that <m>~n! = n \cdot (n-1) \cdot (n-2) .... 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1</m>.  For example, <m>~4! = 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1 = 24</m>.  (<m>~n!</m> is read as "<m>n</m> factorial.")</p>
    <p>Evaluate the following expressions:</p>

        <p><m>1+\dfrac{1}{1!}=</m></p>
        <p><m>1+\dfrac{1}{1!} + \dfrac{1}{2!}=</m></p>
        <p><m>1+\dfrac{1}{1!} + \dfrac{1}{2!} + \dfrac{1}{3!}=</m></p>
        <p><m>1+\dfrac{1}{1!} + \dfrac{1}{2!} + \dfrac{1}{3!} + \dfrac{1}{4!}=</m></p>
        <p><m>1+\dfrac{1}{1!} + \dfrac{1}{2!} + \dfrac{1}{3!} + \dfrac{1}{4!} + \dfrac{1}{5!}=</m></p>
        <p><m>1+\dfrac{1}{1!} + \dfrac{1}{2!} + \dfrac{1}{3!} + \dfrac{1}{4!} + \dfrac{1}{5!} + \dfrac{1}{6!}=</m></p>

    <p>What number does the sum approach as the number of terms increases?</p></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<subsection><title>Checkpoint</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>Why would you expect the graph of <m>y = \ln {x}</m> to lie between the graphs of <m>y = \log_2 {x}</m> and <m>y = \log_3 {x}</m>?</p></li>
    <li><p>What does "ln" stand for in the function <m>y = \ln {x}</m> ?</p></li>
    <li><p>Give decimal approximations for the constants <m>\pi</m> and <m>e</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p>What does <m>~n!</m> stand for?</p></li>
</ol></p></subsection>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Questions for Writing or Discussion</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>If <m>k</m> is negative in <m>P(t) = P_0 e^{kt}</m>, what does this tell you about the graph?</p></li>
    <li><p>To write <m>P(t) = P_0 b^t</m> with base <m>e</m>, we set <m>k = \ln {b}</m>. Why is that?</p></li>
    <li><p>Why is it easier to solve <m>e^t = 30</m> than <m>2^t = 30</m>?</p></li>
    <li><p>Which produces more interest on an investment: annual compounding at rate <m>r</m> or continuous compounding at rate <m>r</m>?</p></li>
</ol></p></assemblage>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Concept Questions</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>Which of the following are irrational numbers?</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>\pi, ~e^2,</m> and <m>2!</m></li>
        <li><m>\pi, ~e^2,</m> and <m>\sqrt{2}</m></li>
        <li><m>\dfrac{23}{24}, 3.14159,</m> and <m>\sqrt{121}</m></li>
        <li><m>3^{15}, ~5^{-3},</m> and <m>\log_3{729}</m></li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>How do we convert from <m>~P(t)=P_0 b^t~</m> to <m>~P(t)=P_0 e^{kt}</m>?</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>b=\ln {k}</m></li>
        <li><m>k=e^b</m></li>
        <li><m>k=\ln {b}</m></li>
        <li><m>kb=e</m></li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>If <m>k \lt 0</m> the graph of <m>y=e^{kx}</m> is</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li>increasing conccave up</li>
        <li>increasing conccave down</li>
        <li>dcreasing conccave up</li>
        <li>dcreasing conccave down</li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>For the function <m>~P(t)= 500 e^{kt}</m>, what is the growth factor?</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>k</m></li>
        <li><m>e^k</m></li>
        <li><m>500e</m></li>
        <li><m>\ln {k}</m></li>
    </ol></p></li>
</ol></p></assemblage>

</section>